        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Pan-Arabism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1880 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan">Jurji Zaydan</a> (1861–1914), who laid its intellectual foundation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> ibn Ali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, who first pressed it as a political project; Constantin Zureiq, Sati’ al-Husri, Zaki al-Arsuzi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>, Syrian thinkers of the 1930s; Aflaq, for long the Ba’ath Party’s chief ideologist; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>, who made Arab nationalism <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>’s state policy</span></div>
