    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pan-Arabism holds that the Arabic-speaking peoples from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> are one people, a view <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan">Jurji Zaydan</a> grounded in their common language rather than religion, race or territory.</li><li>Every attempt at a pan-Arab state failed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the first actual merger of two independent Arab countries, lasted only from 1958 to 1961.</li><li>Its decline is put down to pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a>, sectarian and social divisions, rivalry among Arab leaders and, to a lesser extent, defeat by Israel, above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> kept the name “the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a>” after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> left the union in 1961, implying that it remained open to union with other Arab countries, and renamed itself the Arab Republic of Egypt only in 1973.</p></aside>
    </section>

