    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-hussein-correspondence">Hussein–McMahon Correspondence</a> begins: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> will support Arab independence if the Mashreq <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1916</span><span class="rf-text">The Sykes–Picot Agreement divides parts of the Mashreq between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> instead of forming an independent Arab state</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">League of Arab States</a> is founded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1958</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> merge as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1961</span><span class="rf-text">Syrian army officers carry out a coup and take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1967</span><span class="rf-text">Israel’s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> damages pan-Arabism’s credibility</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">The Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> Union is formed in place of a proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> union</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2024</span><span class="rf-text">The fall of the Assad regime ends Ba’athist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which had espoused pan-Arabism</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz.jpg" alt="Hussein bin Ali, King of Hejaz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Hussein bin Ali, King of Hejaz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sharif_Husayn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The origins of pan-Arabism are often traced to the Nahda, the Arab awakening that flourished in the Arab regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the late 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan">Jurji Zaydan</a> (1861–1914), a Levantine Christian émigré in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, laid much of its intellectual foundation: his articles of the early 20th century argued that the Arabic-speaking regions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> were one people with a shared national consciousness, and his historical novels popularised a secular history of the pre-Islamic and Islamic periods that all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> could claim as their own.</p><p>As a political project it was first pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> ibn Ali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, who sought a unified Arab state in the Mashreq. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-hussein-correspondence">Hussein–McMahon Correspondence</a> of 1915 and 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> agreed to support Arab independence if the Arabs revolted successfully against the Ottomans, but the Sykes–Picot Agreement of 1916 divided parts of the region between Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. When the Ottoman Empire surrendered in 1918, Britain did not keep to the letter of its arrangements with Hussein, and the two powers took guardianship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and Hussein, King of the Hijaz alone, lost his kingdom to the Saudis.</p><p>A more formal ideology was set out in the 1930s by Syrian thinkers: Constantin Zureiq, Sati’ al-Husri, Zaki al-Arsuzi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>. It has been said that Arsuzi was fascinated with the Nazi idea of “racial purity” and that he influenced Aflaq.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan.jpg" alt="Jurji Zaydan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan">Jurji Zaydan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurji_Zaydan_(Edited2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Arabism, in Arabic al-waḥda al-ʿarabiyya, “Arab unity”, espouses the unification of all Arab people in a single nation-state made up of the Arab countries of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. It is closely connected with Arab nationalism, the view that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> constitute a single nation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan">Zaydan</a>’s formulation the linguistic bond among Arabic speakers outweighed religious, racial and particular territorial bonds, and he had a critical influence on the acceptance of a modernised Quranic Arabic, Modern Standard Arabic, as the written and official language of the whole Arab world in place of the local dialects.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>, for long the chief ideologist of the Arab Ba’ath (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>) Party that he and al-Arsuzi helped found, combined elements of Marxist thought with a nationalism that recalled 19th-century European romantic nationalism. Advocates of pan-Arabism have often espoused Arab socialism and strongly opposed Western political involvement in the Arab world, and the ideology sought to strengthen Arab states against outside forces through alliances such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-i-of-jordan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdullah-i-of-jordan.jpg" alt="Abdullah I of Jordan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-i-of-jordan">Abdullah I of Jordan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecil_Beaton_Photographs-_Political_and_Military_Personalities;_Abdullah,_King_of_Jordan;_Abdullah,_King_of_Jordan_CBM1666.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cecil Beaton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Arabism began at the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the most populous Arab country, took little interest in it before the 1950s: in the 1930s and 1940s Egyptian nationalism, not pan-Arabism, was the mode of its political activists. James Jankowski wrote of an “absence of an Arab component in early Egyptian nationalism”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-i-of-jordan">Abdullah I of Jordan</a> dreamed of a Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> uniting Syria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and Jordan under himself; the plan was unpopular among most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and distrust of his ambitions was one of the principal reasons for founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> in 1945. The vision was dropped after his assassination in 1951.</p><p>The British Foreign Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a> called for Arab unity in the 1940s, followed by proposals from pro-British leaders such as Abdullah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuri-al-said">Nuri al-Said</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, but the Egyptian proposals for a broader grouping of independent Arab states prevailed. Only under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> did Arab nationalism, with Arab socialism, become Egypt’s state policy, usually set against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> in neighbouring Israel. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> Nasser gained great popularity among the masses of the Arab world, and pan-Arabism reached its height with Nasserism and Ba’athism in the 1950s and 1960s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="Gamal Abdel Nasser" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Gamal_Abdel_Naser_u_Beogradu,_1962_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Arabism’s record in power is a series of attempted unions, all of which ultimately failed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> of 1958 was the first actual merger of two independent Arab countries. It was formed hastily under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> on the initiative of Syrian leaders who feared a takeover by communists or “reactionaries”, and it was a unitary state, which critics saw as little more than the annexation of a small country by a larger one. In 1961 Syrian army officers staged a coup and withdrew. The Hashemite Arab Federation of Jordan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, founded the same year, collapsed after six months amid tensions with the UAR and the 14 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-states">United Arab States</a>, a confederation of the UAR with the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, dissolved in 1961. In 1963 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Iraq agreed on a federal union that never came about.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> pressed two more projects, the Federation of Arab Republics, which lasted five years, and an Arab Islamic Republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> that never emerged; talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1988 led instead to the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> Union of 1989. The governments of Iraq and Syria were led by rival factions of the Ba’ath Party, which espoused pan-Arabism until the fall of the Assad regime in 2024. The rare lasting unions are the forcible unification of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> by the Saudi rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> in the 1920s, the United Arab Emirates and the unification of North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Religious scholars and sheikhs across the Arab countries, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, held that pan-Arabism contradicted pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a>, the ideal of a united Islamic community, the ummah: by promoting only Arab unity and ideals, not Islamic ones, it separated itself from the ummah. The religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> of these societies helped pan-Islamism prevail. Sectarian and social differences, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-shia-divide">Sunni–Shia divide</a> and the clash between secular states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and religious Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, raised doubts about whether the project was viable at all.</p><p>Rival leaders competed to be the voice of the Arab world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a>, meant as the spearhead of pan-Arabism, did not receive the expected praise from other Arab states, especially in the Gulf, and its critics called it an annexation. Abdullah’s Greater Syria had bred distrust of a similar kind. To a lesser extent, the defeats by Israel, and above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967, together with the failure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Syria to generate economic growth, damaged the ideology’s credibility.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>“By the mid-1970s,” according to The Continuum Political Encyclopedia of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, “the idea of Arab unity became less and less apparent in Arab politics, though it remained a wishful goal among the masses.” By the late 1980s pan-Arabism was being eclipsed by nationalist and Islamist ideologies. Although it had lost its appeal by the 1990s, it continued to exercise an intellectual hegemony throughout the Arab world.</p><p>Something of the founders’ project outlasted the politics: Modern Standard Arabic, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurji-zaydan">Zaydan</a> helped establish, is the written and official language throughout the Arab world, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">League of Arab States</a>, founded in 1945, grew out of the same search for unity. Ba’athist rule carried the ideology into the 21st century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> until the fall of the Assad regime in 2024.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pan-Arabism is the ideology of uniting all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in one state, born in the Ottoman Arab lands in the late 19th century and first pressed as a political project by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. It reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> and the Ba’ath parties in the 1950s and 1960s, but every attempted union, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> of 1958 to 1961, failed, and from the late 1980s it was eclipsed by nationalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a>.</em></p>

